1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,661
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,661 --> 00:00:06,723
This is chapter thirty: concurrent collections.

3
00:00:07,173 --> 00:00:09,382
The star is ConcurrentHashMap.

4
00:00:09,382 --> 00:00:18,885
Using it well comes down to two things: understanding why it's fast, and always making a compound operation a single atomic call.

5
00:00:19,785 --> 00:00:22,713
Here's a correct inventory reservation.

6
00:00:22,713 --> 00:00:26,092
A plain HashMap behind a synchronized method.

7
00:00:26,092 --> 00:00:31,198
It works, but it's one global lock across two hundred thousand SKUs.

8
00:00:31,648 --> 00:00:36,545
A thread reserving milk blocks a thread reserving rice, for no reason.

9
00:00:36,545 --> 00:00:42,350
Under heavy checkout load, throughput collapses to about one reservation at a time.

10
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:48,856
Collections dot synchronizedMap doesn't help, because it's the same single lock moved inside.

11
00:00:48,856 --> 00:00:52,213
What we want is contention per key, not per map.

12
00:00:53,113 --> 00:00:56,605
ConcurrentHashMap guards each bin independently.

13
00:00:56,605 --> 00:01:01,988
Writing to an empty bin is a single compare-and-swap, with no lock at all.

14
00:01:02,438 --> 00:01:07,515
Writing to an occupied bin synchronizes on that bin's first node only.

15
00:01:07,965 --> 00:01:09,431
And reads never lock.

16
00:01:09,431 --> 00:01:17,736
The table and node fields are volatile, so a reader always sees a consistent view, even during a write to the same bin.

17
00:01:18,186 --> 00:01:21,041
So writes to different bins never interact.

18
00:01:21,041 --> 00:01:27,613
There's no global state, which is exactly why size is approximate and iteration can't be fail-fast.

19
00:01:27,613 --> 00:01:29,007
And it rejects nulls.

20
00:01:29,007 --> 00:01:38,965
In a concurrent map, get returning null could mean absent or mapped to null, and you could never check which, because the map might change in between.

21
00:01:39,865 --> 00:01:45,353
This bug survives code review because every individual call is thread-safe.

22
00:01:45,353 --> 00:01:47,402
Check containsKey, then put.

23
00:01:47,402 --> 00:01:51,134
But two atomic calls don't make an atomic sequence.

24
00:01:51,134 --> 00:01:55,818
Two threads both see the key missing, both put, and one is lost.

25
00:01:55,818 --> 00:01:58,745
The fix is one atomic call, putIfAbsent.

26
00:01:59,195 --> 00:02:06,813
The rule: if your logic reads the map and then writes based on what it read, use a single atomic operation.

27
00:02:06,813 --> 00:02:16,923
putIfAbsent, a conditional replace or remove, or compute, computeIfAbsent and merge, which run the whole read-modify-write under the bin lock.

28
00:02:17,823 --> 00:02:21,811
Here's the reservation rewritten with no lock of our own.

29
00:02:21,811 --> 00:02:28,387
compute runs the check and the decrement atomically, holding only the lock for this SKU's bin.

30
00:02:28,387 --> 00:02:32,585
If there isn't enough stock, it returns the value unchanged.

31
00:02:33,035 --> 00:02:37,624
Now two threads reserving different SKUs never touch each other.

32
00:02:37,624 --> 00:02:45,942
Two threads reserving the same SKU serialise on one bin, which is exactly the serialisation the business rule needs.

33
00:02:46,842 --> 00:02:54,670
computeIfAbsent makes a cache that loads each key at most once, even when many threads miss at the same moment.

34
00:02:54,670 --> 00:03:03,909
The naive get, then put, runs the expensive evaluation once per contending thread, which is the stampede a cache exists to prevent.

35
00:03:04,359 --> 00:03:08,522
But the mapping function runs while holding the bin's lock.

36
00:03:08,522 --> 00:03:11,274
So never modify the same map inside it.

37
00:03:11,274 --> 00:03:16,284
ConcurrentHashMap detects that and throws a recursive update exception.

38
00:03:16,284 --> 00:03:23,623
And never do slow I/O inside it, because every other key hashing to that bin waits on your network call.

39
00:03:24,073 --> 00:03:27,802
For expensive loads, store a CompletableFuture instead.

40
00:03:27,802 --> 00:03:33,566
The lock is held only long enough to register the in-flight work, not to complete it.

41
00:03:34,466 --> 00:03:37,215
A thread-safe map with unsafe contents.

42
00:03:37,215 --> 00:03:42,642
computeIfAbsent is atomic, but the add that follows happens outside the lock.

43
00:03:42,642 --> 00:03:46,518
Two threads can hold the same ArrayList and corrupt it.

44
00:03:46,518 --> 00:03:53,003
Use a concurrent value type, like ConcurrentLinkedQueue, or swap immutable lists with merge.

45
00:03:53,003 --> 00:03:56,738
For per-key counters, a LongAdder per key works well.

46
00:03:57,188 --> 00:04:05,803
Remember: the map safely publishes the reference it stores, but it guarantees nothing about the object on the other end of it.

47
00:04:05,803 --> 00:04:10,863
And bulk operations like putAll and clear are thread-safe, but not atomic.

48
00:04:10,863 --> 00:04:13,188
A reader can see half of a putAll.

49
00:04:14,088 --> 00:04:16,429
Inside, resizing is cooperative.

50
00:04:16,429 --> 00:04:21,402
As bins move to the new table, they're replaced by forwarding nodes.

51
00:04:21,402 --> 00:04:25,937
A writer that hits one helps migrate a stretch of bins itself.

52
00:04:25,937 --> 00:04:30,472
Readers are simply redirected, so resizing never blocks reads.

53
00:04:30,922 --> 00:04:36,588
There's no single size counter, because it would be a contention point on every write.

54
00:04:36,588 --> 00:04:41,200
Instead it's striped across cells, like LongAdder, and size sums them.

55
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:44,560
So it's a value that was true at no single instant.

56
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:47,327
Use it for metrics, never for correctness.

57
00:04:47,777 --> 00:04:50,169
And iterators are weakly consistent.

58
00:04:50,169 --> 00:04:58,272
They never throw ConcurrentModificationException, never skip an element that was present throughout, and never repeat one.

59
00:04:58,272 --> 00:05:00,065
But they're not a snapshot.

60
00:05:00,515 --> 00:05:02,587
This is the Java 8 rewrite.

61
00:05:02,587 --> 00:05:06,422
Java 7 used sixteen segments with their own locks.

62
00:05:06,422 --> 00:05:11,715
If you see code tuning concurrencyLevel, it's now only a sizing hint.

63
00:05:12,615 --> 00:05:13,877
Production rules.

64
00:05:13,877 --> 00:05:17,588
Make every read-modify-write a single atomic call.

65
00:05:17,588 --> 00:05:23,154
Use computeIfAbsent for load-once caches, and store futures for slow loads.

66
00:05:23,154 --> 00:05:25,825
Keep values concurrent or immutable.

67
00:05:25,825 --> 00:05:28,942
Use LongAdder values for per-key counters.

68
00:05:28,942 --> 00:05:33,543
And for a concurrent set, use ConcurrentHashMap dot newKeySet.

69
00:05:33,993 --> 00:05:36,469
Never write containsKey then put.

70
00:05:36,469 --> 00:05:40,744
Never do I/O inside compute, or touch the same map there.

71
00:05:40,744 --> 00:05:43,444
Never share mutable values unsafely.

72
00:05:43,444 --> 00:05:46,144
Never make a decision based on size.

73
00:05:46,144 --> 00:05:48,095
And never put nulls in it.

74
00:05:48,995 --> 00:05:49,673
To recap.

75
00:05:49,673 --> 00:05:57,737
ConcurrentHashMap locks per bin: a CAS for empty bins, synchronized for occupied ones, and lock-free reads.

76
00:05:57,737 --> 00:06:03,238
Two atomic calls don't make an atomic sequence, so use compute and merge.

77
00:06:03,238 --> 00:06:08,740
computeIfAbsent holds the bin lock, so no I/O and no recursion inside it.

78
00:06:08,740 --> 00:06:12,056
And a safe map doesn't make its values safe.

79
00:06:12,506 --> 00:06:15,425
Next is chapter thirty-one: virtual threads.

80
00:06:15,425 --> 00:06:16,751
Thanks for watching.
